# Scandal Season — Season Six, Chapter 21: "The Broker"

**STATUS: FULL DRAFT** — awaiting Beth review.

**Format key:** `[D]` dialogue-heavy scene · `[F]` fashion beat (2–3 diegetic options, no coin cost) · `[C]` dressing ritual (direction + twenty 5-coin pins, 21 turns) · `[T]` turn-dense beat · untagged plot beat. `★ KEY DECISION n/3` — exactly three per chapter, parser-critical syntax.

**Turns:** ordinary scenes run 7–10 turns; the `[C]` ritual runs 21 turns; Key Decisions run 3 turns; the S39 Gazette sting runs 1 turn; the S40 cliffhanger runs 8 turns.

**Canon applied:** Season Six storyboard (Act III, "The Reckoning") is highest authority; ten-season plot arc; Season Six Chapters 1–20. Locked money state entering: venture working capital £22,736 · Hartwell face reclaimed £36,500 · outstanding £36,500 of the locked £73,000 · cash in hand £72 10s · Hartwell paper held at cost £12,316 · cumulative venture P&L −£1,948, the venture's trading closed. This chapter: Mr. Drayton names himself — the estate's broker, eleven years Rackham's man, precise, unhurried; the estate's offer, £21,000 face (the £15,000 Dealing plus £6,000 of the retained market paper) for £14,000 AND the acknowledgment — the estate's sealed document and Rose's public naming, spoken where the market hears and written in the buyback book; the City's rumor — *Rackham lives* — answered plain by Drayton, early: the man is eleven years buried; what lives is his paper and his sealed word, opened Tuesday. The house accepts in principle — the assignment and the £14,000 fall due Tuesday. Closing state: venture £22,736 · face reclaimed £36,500 · outstanding £36,500 · cash £72 10s. The Crown's £9,000 held through trustees; Drummond cold. Sloane unbeaten; the duel's touch-count stands at 27 going into the week. Pyke and Cecilia do not appear. Lucien de Valcourt is absence-framed, never on-page.

**Occasion:** Monday — the broker's week; the estate's offer on the table, the acknowledgment priced, the City's rumor answered.

**What changes for Rose:** The paper lives — Rackham's sealed word comes to the City through his named broker, and the £21,000 parcel comes with a price that isn't money; the house accepts, and Tuesday opens his sealed word in the coffee-room.

## Chapter 21 — "The Broker"

### L21.S1 · [F] · Rose (alone)
*Purpose: the week dressed — Monday morning; the broker's week, the named; the chosen. (Turns: 8)*
> *(Monday morning, her chambers. The broker's week wants its cloth — the offer's week, the named man's — and the wardrobe gets the first word.)*
> The maids have laid out the grey silk and the blue twill. Rose stands in her shift and weighs them the way she weighs paper: for what each one will buy.
> (T1 · look closer) *The grey is quiet, the blue is believed — and the week's first reading has to be both.*
> "The blue." She steps into the twill. "The broker names himself today, and I want the City's belief on before he speaks."
> (T2 · tone) *Dry, decided, and already across the table in her head — Monday wants the offer, and the offer wants blue.*
> The corset laced true, the twill over it, the buttons done by her own hands. In the glass: a woman dressed for arithmetic, not theatre.
> (T3 · stance) *Upright, plain, unhurried — the broker's woman dresses the broker's way.*
> She pins the white heather at her waist. The week's mark, back for the new week. The City has seen it every day of the half's closing; let it see it now.
> (T4 · remembered micro-decision) *Keep the heather. The mark's hers, and the week's business is hers too.*
> The hair drawn back, severe. Nothing loose. Today is a working day the way last Tuesday was — terms, heard whole.
> (T5 · look closer) *The glass gives her back the week's face: level, unadorned, ready for a man who deals in paper.*
> "The broker." She says it to the mirror. "Named, today. We'll see what a name costs."
> (T6 · dialogue) *"The blue's believed."; "The named, today."; "The week's begun."*
> The shawl — deep blue, Élise's fitting — settled over her shoulders. The carriage already called below.
> (T7 · choice — no coin cost, remembered) *the blue twill — the believed, for the naming (the City believes the blue, and Monday wants belief); the grey silk — the endured, held back for the opening's morrow; the heather — pinned, the week's mark, or left plain, the morning unadorned.* — remembered: *The blue, worn.*
> At the door she pauses, hand on the frame, and looks back at the grey silk on its stand. Tomorrow, perhaps. Today is blue's day.
> (T8 · remembered micro-decision) *Bank the morning's resolve with the week's cloth — blue for the naming, the offer faced.*
> *The morning light takes the blue twill without argument — Monday dressed, the broker's week begun.*
